{
  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Losartan, through its inclusion in the class of Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists, may be associated with a treatment effect on Coronary Artery Disease. This is supported by the fact that both Valsartan and Telmisartan, which are also members of this class, are known to treat Coronary Artery Disease. The shared pathway from Losartan to these drugs, through the lowest common ancestor of Losartan and Valsartan, and then to Coronary Artery Disease, indicates a potential mechanism by which Losartan could exert a therapeutic effect on this condition. The consistent scores across the three paths, all indicating a final score of 0.6997, suggest a strong and reliable relationship in the context of the dataset. The evidence points to a possible pharmacological mechanism involving the modulation of angiotensin II signaling, which is a well-established pathway in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease. However, the exact mechanism by which Losartan influences this condition remains to be fully elucidated, and further research is needed to confirm the therapeutic potential of Losartan in the treatment of Coronary Artery Disease.",
